4. Color fade
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 Food companies are under pressure to remove artificial colors from their food. Photo: Kevin Carter/Getty Images


 
 &nbsp; 
 
 The quickest way for food companies to alleviate pressure to ditch artificial colors in their products might be to just ditch colors entirely.

 State of play: Makers of products like freezer pops and sports drinks may remove colors altogether as they face pressure from the Trump administration and the MAHA movement to remove synthetic dyes, Bloomberg reports .

 Part of the concern is that the natural ingredients have a shorter shelf life and "are likely to get more expensive as more of the industry reaches for them." 🎨 Yes, but: Not everyone is ready to give up on colors.

 Mars has about 100 employees working on its plan to eliminate artificial colors from M&amp;Ms — with about a quarter solely dedicated to trying to replicate the color blue with natural ingredients, WSJ reported in June. 💭 Nathan's thought bubble: I wonder whether this push will fade if and when RFK leaves the administration.


 
 
